e right after Black's H6 had made the position close."}, {"turn": 34, "text": "J7 extended White's wall along the seventh row, but it lost 27.4 points and sent Black's lead from +7.5 to +34.9. The engine wanted G2, one of many moves where it asked for that point and didn't get it."}, {"turn": 35, "text": "The 20k answered with J6, the worst move of their game. It lost 28.9 points and handed back nearly everything, with Black's lead dropping from +34.9 to +6.0. The two biggest errors of the game came on consecutive moves."}, {"turn": 36, "text": "E2 matched the engine's first choice and gained 3.3 points, moving the lead from +6.0 to +2.7. It was a rare clean answer in a messy stretch, and it kept White in the fight around the lower-left corner."}, {"turn": 44, "text": "With the lead at -12.6, White had a chance at the lower-left corner, but B1 lost 55.3 points and sent the lead to +42.7. The engine wanted G2. This decided the game: by the final position the White stones in that corner are gone, Black holds A1, C1 and D1, and Black has made 14 captures."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "Everything it ran was reading and writing records or inspecting the current position: the `inspect` function and `current-position-liberties.js` parse the board and list chains, liberties and empty regions, `play` just relays a move the player chose, and the later scripts rebuild the SGF, verify captures against the final board, and write notes. None of it loops over candidate moves or plays out continuations, so the player chose every move itself.", "stop": false}}